<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Sample input file for PDE — minimum boiling azeotrope, binary A-B system.

  Energy form: HS
    G(x, T) = H(x) - T·S(x)

  System description:
    - Two components: A (x=0) and B (x=1)
    - One gas (vapor) phase; one liquid phase
    - Symmetric minimum boiling azeotrope at x = 0.5

  Key temperatures:
    T_b,A = T_b,B = 400 K   (pure-component boiling points, symmetric)
    T_az         = 350 K    (azeotrope; minimum of the boiling-point curve)

  Why a concave G_liquid does NOT produce an azeotrope
  ======================================================
  A concave G_liquid (negative x² coefficient) causes the lower convex hull
  to draw a single chord across the entire liquid range.  The vapor curve
  never dips below that chord, so the hull sees only one wide two-phase band
  (ordinary VLE) rather than two narrow lobes converging at x_az.

  Design that does produce an azeotrope
  ========================================
  Both G curves are upward-opening parabolas (convex) with their minimum at
  x_az = 0.5.  The vapor parabola is steeper (A_V = 4 > A_L = 1) and shifted
  upward so that:

    • G_V(0.5, T) < G_L(0.5, T)  for T > 350 K   ← vapor stable at x_az
    • G_L(x, T)  < G_V(x, T)     at x = 0, 1      for T < 400 K  ← liquid
      stable at the pure endpoints below their boiling points

  At T between 350 K and 400 K the lower convex hull therefore has:
    liquid → [L+V tangent] → vapor (around x = 0.5) → [V+L tangent] → liquid

  This produces two two-phase lobes converging to the azeotrope point at 350 K.
  Below 350 K the G_V parabola rises above G_L everywhere and only liquid is
  stable.  Above 400 K only vapor is stable.

  HS coefficients
  ===============
  G_L(x,T) = A_L·(x−0.5)² + H_L0 − T·S_L
            = x² − x + 0.25 − 0.005·T
    H_L: x0 = 0.25,  x1 = −1.0,  x2 = 1.0
    S_L: x0 = 0.005

  G_V(x,T) = A_V·(x−0.5)² + H_V0 − T·S_V
            = 4x² − 4x + 6.25 − 0.02·T
    H_V: x0 = 6.25,  x1 = −4.0,  x2 = 4.0
    S_V: x0 = 0.02

  Verification
  ============
  Azeotrope tangency (G_L = G_V and dG_L/dx = dG_V/dx at x=0.5, T=350):
    G_L(0.5, 350) = 0  −  1.75 = −1.75
    G_V(0.5, 350) = 5.25 − 7.00 = −1.75  ✓
    Both slopes = 0 at x = 0.5 (parabola minima)  ✓

  Pure boiling points (G_L = G_V at x=0 and x=1, T=400):
    G_L(0, 400) = 0.25 − 2.00 = −1.75
    G_V(0, 400) = 6.25 − 8.00 = −1.75  ✓
    (Symmetric, so x=1 gives the same result.)  ✓

  Below T_az (T = 300 K): discriminant of G_L = G_V is negative → no crossing
    → liquid stable everywhere  ✓
-->

<pde>

  <system>
    <components>A B</components>
    <energy_form>HS</energy_form>
  </system>

  <temperature>
    <min>250</min>
    <max>500</max>
    <initial>500</initial>
  </temperature>

  <!-- ============================================================
       Gas (vapor) phase — steeper parabola, minimum at x = 0.5
       H_V(x) = 4x² − 4x + 6.25   (min value 5.25 at x=0.5)
       S_V(x) = 0.02                (high entropy → stable at high T)
       The large S_V makes G_V decrease rapidly with T, passing below
       G_L first near x=0.5 as T rises above 350 K.
       ============================================================ -->

  <phase name="vapor" type="gas">
    <energy model="quadratic">
      <H x0="6.25" x1="-4.0" x2="4.0"/>
      <S x0="0.02" x1="0.0" x2="0.0"/>
    </energy>
  </phase>

  <!-- ============================================================
       Liquid phase — shallower parabola, minimum at x = 0.5
       H_L(x) = x² − x + 0.25   (min value 0 at x=0.5)
       S_L(x) = 0.005            (low entropy → stable at low T)
       ============================================================ -->

  <phase name="liquid" type="liquid">
    <energy model="quadratic">
      <H x0="0.25" x1="-1.0" x2="1.0"/>
      <S x0="0.005" x1="0.0" x2="0.0"/>
    </energy>
  </phase>

</pde>
